{"language_code":"eng","language_probability":0.9571603536605835,"text":"Google just pulled off the biggest heist in Internet history, and nobody's calling it out. For twenty years, the deal was simple: You write a blog, Google sends people, everyone eats. Then AI Overviews showed up. Now Google scrapes your article, answers the query at the top of the page, and the user never clicks. Studies are showing organic click-through rates collapsing by up to thirty percent on informational queries. Some publishers report sixty percent drops overnight. The content still gets used. The traffic just vanishes. Think about what that means. Google trained on your words for free. Now it resells your words as an answer and keeps the ad revenue your blog used to earn. This is why everyone's suddenly obsessed with podcasts, Substacks, and niche YouTubers with fifty thousand subs — not because they're authentic, because they're the last places on the Internet where a human actually has to click through to consume the thing. The open web didn't die from TikTok. It died the moment Google realized it didn't need you to send the traffic anywhere. It could just answer the question itself using everything you ever wrote. The next billion-dollar wave isn't SEO. It's wherever Google's AI can't follow.","words":[{"text":"Google","start":0.12,"end":0.38,"type":"word","logprob":-0.0015068616485223174},{"text":" ","start":0.38,"end":0.5,"type":"spacing","logprob":-0.00001168244216387393},{"text":"just","start":0.5,"end":0.68,"type":"word","logprob":-0.00001168244216387393},{"text":" ","start":0.68,"end":0.76,"type":"spacing","logprob":-5.364403477869928e-6},{"text":"pulled","start":0.76,"end":0.96,"type":"word","logprob":-5.364403477869928e-6},{"text":" ","start":0.96,"end":1.02,"type":"spacing","logprob":0.0},{"text":"off","start":1.02,"end":1.2,"type":"word","logprob":0.0},{"text":" ","start":1.2,"end":1.24,"type":"spacing","logprob":-3.4570634852570947e-6},{"text":"the","start":1.24,"end":1.3